  "summary": "Overnight markets repriced a more aggressive Federal Reserve path after October consumer prices rose 0.4 percent, more than expected. Treasury yields jumped, with the two-year at 4.73 percent and the ten-year at 4.98 percent, while December SOFR futures imply about 4.28 percent against a 3.88 percent SOFR fixing. Equities absorbed the move better than bonds: the S&P 500 is near 7521, the Nasdaq near 25066, and the VIX near 20. Commodity marks are firm, with WTI near 86 dollars, Brent near 91.50, and gold futures near 4030, after Brent only briefly traded under 90. The dollar is mixed: euro-dollar is 1.1214 and dollar-yen is near 158.2. Australian dollar-kiwi near 1.227 broke its recent range lower and remains a rich multi-year percentile. Missing non-US consumer and labor series are a research caveat, not a trading ban, and kiwi spot does not need a New Zealand yield curve. Next up are US producer prices and the euro-area GDP flash on November 13, then Canadian CPI and Japanese GDP on November 16.",
  "type": "OVERNIGHT_ACCEPTED_PUBLIC_NEWS"
}
